@meta
id: 01-12
title: Nothing here is unrecoverable
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: cmdline, esc, insert-i, insert-a, survival-motion
par: 32
minutes: 5
vscode: closing the tab and answering "Don't Save"
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
The thing that keeps people tentative in vim for far too long is a
suspicion that they might do something they cannot take back. You cannot.
Everything in this chapter is recoverable with a command line and one or
two keys.

Two of them, today:

  `:q!` quits and throws away every change since the last write. The file
  on disk is untouched. This is the answer when you have made a mess and
  the mess is not worth sorting out.

  `:e!` reloads the file from disk into the window you are already in.
  Same effect on the changes, except you stay where you are and carry on.

Prove it to yourself. This lesson asks you to break the file on purpose
first.

  1. On the top line, type some nonsense into the middle of the heading.
  2. Run `:e!`. The heading comes back.
  3. Now do the real job: the last bullet should end `capped at three`.

Then save and quit.

@keys
| `:e!` | reload the file from disk, discarding changes |
| `:q!` | quit, discarding changes |
| `ZQ` | the same as `:q!`, without the colon |
| `:w` | write — the point after which `:e!` cannot help you |

@hints
Any nonsense will do for step one. `i` then a few letters then `Esc`. You
want to see it land in the file before you undo the damage.
--
`:e!` and Enter. Vim reloads the buffer from disk. The `[+]` flag goes
away with it, because the window and the file now match again.
--
The bullet is the last line. `$a` then ` three`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    i xxx <Esc> :e!<Enter>
    j j j j $ a <space> three <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The exclamation mark is worth reading as a word. In vim command lines it
means "yes, I know, do it anyway" — `:q!` is quit-anyway, `:e!`
reload-anyway, and that reading holds for commands you have not met yet.
Vim refuses first and offers you the `!` second, which is a reasonable
way for an editor to behave.

Both of these commands stop at the last `:w`. They take you back to what
is on disk, not further. Chapter 2 hands you `u`, which walks back through
your changes one at a time and does not care when you last saved, and
that is the tool you will actually use fifty times a day. This pair is for
the bigger decision: this session was a mistake, start again.

Worth saying plainly, because it is the whole point of the chapter: there
is no state you can type yourself into here that `Esc` `Esc` `:e!` does
not get you out of.

